# prefer-fewer-comments -- rules
#
# Everything here is optional: with this file empty, the hook runs on the
# defaults below. Two kinds of line are understood.
#
# 1. Budget overrides, written as `key = number`:
#
#      max-comment-lines   = 2      lines one comment may span
#      max-comment-chars   = 240    characters one comment may run to
#      max-doc-lines       = 5      lines one docstring may span
#      max-doc-chars       = 600    characters one docstring may run to
#      max-comment-ratio   = 0.30   share of added lines that may be comments
#      min-lines-for-ratio = 20     smallest change the ratio applies to
#
#    Docstrings get the larger budget on purpose: a signature contract has to
#    live next to the signature, while an explanation of the code does not.
#
# 2. Exemptions, written as gitignore-style patterns -- files that may carry
#    as much commentary as they like:
#
#      *    any run of characters within one path segment
#      ?    a single character within one path segment
#      **   any number of path segments (a/**/b also matches a/b)
#
#    A pattern containing a slash is anchored at the project root; one without
#    matches the basename at any depth. A trailing slash covers a directory
#    and everything under it. The last matching pattern wins, and a leading
#    `!` puts a path back under the budget.
#
# Two locations are read, in order: the hook's own directory (a `--user`
# install keeps global rules there) and the project's `.claude/` or `.codex/`
# directory, so project rules have the last word.
#
# Examples -- delete the `#` to use one:
#
# max-comment-lines = 3
# max-comment-ratio = 0.4
# docs/
# **/*.md
# examples/**
# vendor/
# !vendor/our-fork/**
